      {
        "id": "the-engine-was-not-the-only-question-metal-fragments-could-have-damaged-the-turbo-and-other-injectors",
        "title": "The engine was not the only question. Metal fragments could have damaged the turbo and other injectors.",
        "description": "With metal fragments throughout cylinder three, the specialist could not know whether the turbo had been damaged or the remaining injectors were at risk without further inspection.",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": "h3",
            "id": "",
            "text": "Path A"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Replace only the engine — take risk on existing turbo and remaining injectors."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "£5,000"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "If turbo fails later: add £800–£1,500 + labour. If another injector fails: add £500–£800 per injector + potential engine damage."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": "h3",
            "id": "",
            "text": "Path B"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Replace turbo preventatively and fit a full set of new injectors."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "£6,800"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Eliminates both risks. The upfront cost is higher, but downtime for future failures would cost thousands in lost revenue."
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "src": "/case-studies/assets/5.1-mercedes-sprinter-injector-metal-path.png",
            "alt": "Mercedes OM651 metal fragment path diagram showing debris from injector failure reaching turbocharger",
            "width": "",
            "height": ""
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The fleet owner chose Path B. The upfront cost was higher, but downtime for a future turbo or injector failure would cost thousands in lost revenue. Key takeaway: when an injector fails catastrophically, replace the full set."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Fork in the road"
          }
        ],
